My old man used to run his own computer repair business.

Tiberian Sun somehow made it's way onto one of his master disk ghost images.

Everytime he rebuilt someones computer, I knew just where to find it. https://t.co/zaT3xif9HL

Arx Fatalis was given to me as a free CD by one of his buddies that ran a shop full of PC hardware.

It's single player, but my brother and I would play on two adjacent computers to pretend like it was split screen as we journeyed through the caverns and crypts.

Day of Defeat @_alecdj and I spent hours fucking around with config files and folders trying to get a mod working to created bots (sturmbots was the package from memory).

Once we solved it, we burned the finished folder onto a master CD so we could always get it working again.

@_alecdj Age of empires I I'm pretty sure came from a CD that was inside a Kellogs Corn Flakes box. I think AOE II was the first PC game I ever paid for.

Never learned the strategy, just build big ass fortresses and allowed my fully upgraded castles to mow down hordes of soldiers.

@_alecdj Heretic I actually just played through again. I could never finish it as a kid, but it was a tough choice between it and the original Wolfenstein for this list.

@_alecdj Team Fortess II I believe is responsible for my highest hour count of any game ever.

They just got the balance perfectly right on 2fort.

I also went deep into the item and hats trading game. Looking back, taught me a lot about money, inflation, and negotiating deals.

@_alecdj Tony Hawks 4 was always my favourite in the series, and it wasn't close.

Countless hours building epic skateparks that you could grind around like a rollercoaster...only to fall into a pit of spikes at the end.

Engineering and all that...

